$ jailops host certificate show
Certificate of this host: self-signed (generated by the daemon)
  Subject:     CN=alpha.example.org
  Names:       alpha.example.org
  Issuer:      CN=alpha.example.org
  Valid until: 2036-01-31 09:00 UTC (3620 days left)
  Fingerprint: <fingerprint>
  Trust:       no public CA signed it: clients pin the fingerprint
  Listener:    port 39110

Clients add this host with an API token (jot_..., on standard input):
  jailops remote add alpha https://alpha.example.org:39110 --fingerprint <sha256> --token-stdin
or with a single-use trust token from 'jailops trust add' (it pins this certificate):
  jailops remote add alpha.example.org:39110 --token-stdin

$ jailops host certificate self-signed
host-certificate/self-signed already served, nothing to do

$ jailops host certificate acme --domain console.example.com
! usage: ACME needs your agreement to the CA's terms of service: read them, then pass --accept-tos
! hint: run 'jailops host certificate acme --help' for usage
[exit 2]

$ jailops host certificate acme --domain *.example.com --accept-tos
! unsupported: "*.example.com" is a wildcard name: wildcards need the dns-01 challenge, which JailOps does not offer
! hint: list each name the console is reached by, or use your own certificate (upload one)
[exit 6]

$ jailops host certificate acme --domain console.example.com --email admin@example.com --accept-tos --staging --dry-run
dry-run: would turn ACME on for the console's certificate
enabled: true
email: admin@example.com
domains:
  - console.example.com
directory_url: https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory
challenge: http-01
accept_tos: true

$ jailops host certificate acme --domain console.example.com --email admin@example.com --accept-tos --staging
host-certificate/acme requested for console.example.com; the self-signed certificate is served until the CA answers
! The CA reaches the daemon only through a redirect: add these lines to the translation section of /etc/pf.conf:
!     rdr pass inet proto tcp from any to (self) port 80 -> 127.0.0.1 port 39080
!     rdr pass inet6 proto tcp from any to (self) port 80 -> ::1 port 39080
! Follow the request with: jailops host certificate show

$ jailops host certificate show
Certificate of this host: ACME (obtained and renewed from a public CA)
  Subject:     CN=alpha.example.org
  Names:       alpha.example.org
  Issuer:      CN=alpha.example.org
  Valid until: 2036-01-31 09:00 UTC (3620 days left)
  Fingerprint: <fingerprint>
  Trust:       no public CA signed it: clients pin the fingerprint
  Listener:    port 39110

ACME
  State:       requesting
  Domains:     console.example.com
  Challenge:   http-01 (port 80, redirected to 39080)
  Directory:   staging (untrusted certificates, for trying the setup)
  Terms:       https://ca.example.com/terms
  Redirect:    add these lines to the translation section of /etc/pf.conf:
      rdr pass inet proto tcp from any to (self) port 80 -> 127.0.0.1 port 39080
      rdr pass inet6 proto tcp from any to (self) port 80 -> ::1 port 39080

Clients add this host with an API token (jot_..., on standard input):
  jailops remote add console https://console.example.com:39110 --fingerprint <sha256> --token-stdin
or with a single-use trust token from 'jailops trust add' (it pins this certificate):
  jailops remote add alpha.example.org:39110 --token-stdin

$ jailops host certificate upload --cert <dir>/console.example.org.crt
! usage: give the certificate chain with --cert FILE and its key with --key FILE
! hint: run 'jailops host certificate upload --help' for usage
[exit 2]

$ jailops host certificate upload --cert <dir>/console.example.org.crt --key <dir>/console.example.org.key --hostname www.example.org
! invalid: the certificate is not valid for www.example.org
[exit 2]

$ jailops host certificate upload --cert <dir>/console.example.org.crt --key <dir>/console.example.org.key
host-certificate/uploaded for console.example.org, valid until 2026-06-01
! Clients add this host with: jailops remote add alpha https://alpha.example.org:39110 --fingerprint <sha256> --token-stdin

$ jailops host certificate show
Certificate of this host: uploaded
  Subject:     CN=console.example.org
  Names:       console.example.org
  Issuer:      CN=console.example.org
  Valid until: 2026-06-01 09:00 UTC (92 days left)
  Fingerprint: <fingerprint>
  Trust:       no public CA signed it: clients pin the fingerprint
  Listener:    port 39110

Clients add this host with an API token (jot_..., on standard input):
  jailops remote add alpha https://alpha.example.org:39110 --fingerprint <sha256> --token-stdin
or with a single-use trust token from 'jailops trust add' (it pins this certificate):
  jailops remote add console.example.org:39110 --token-stdin

$ jailops host certificate self-signed -y
! This replaces the uploaded certificate for console.example.org (valid until 2026-06-01) with the self-signed one:
! browsers warn again and every client has to pin the new fingerprint.
! Type the name "console.example.org" to confirm: 
! usage: confirmation required: standard input is not a terminal and gave no answer
! hint: type the name on standard input, or pass --i-know (-y does not answer this question)
[exit 2]

$ jailops host certificate self-signed
! This replaces the uploaded certificate for console.example.org (valid until 2026-06-01) with the self-signed one:
! browsers warn again and every client has to pin the new fingerprint.
! Type the name "console.example.org" to confirm: aborted: the name did not match, nothing was changed
[exit 1]

$ jailops host certificate self-signed --i-know
host-certificate/self-signed served, fingerprint <fingerprint>

$ jailops host certificate show -o json
{
  "kind": "HostCertificate",
  "source": "self-signed",
  "current": {
    "subject": "CN=alpha.example.org",
    "issuer": "CN=alpha.example.org",
    "names": [
      "alpha.example.org"
    ],
    "not_before": "2026-01-30T09:00:00Z",
    "not_after": "2036-01-31T09:00:00Z",
    "days_left": 3620,
    "fingerprint": "<sha256>",
    "self_signed": true,
    "public_ca": false
  },
  "configured": false,
  "listener": true,
  "port": 39110,
  "remote_add": "jailops remote add alpha https://alpha.example.org:39110 --fingerprint <sha256> --token-stdin"
}

